  "query_preview": "..\n(Tuesday morning through next Saturday)\nIssued at 226 PM EDT Sun Oct 11 2026\n\nA tale of two air masses for the long term forecast. Through the \nmiddle of the week into Friday, subtropical ridge will build in \nover the CWA that will bring very warm and (for October) \nrelatively moist air mass to the Southeast. Highs Tuesday through \nFriday will be well into the mid to upper 80s and a few 90s may \neven creep into parts of central Georgia. Dewpoints will be well \ninto the 60s area-wide. These highs will be 10 to as much as 20 \ndegrees above average. Depending on the day, we might approach \nrecord highs at a few sites. Overnight lows will be in the upper \n50s to low 60s. These are unlikely to approach record minimum \nhighs, but will still be 5-15 degrees above average for October. \nRain chances look to be near zero through this time period.\n\nInto Friday, some significant changes look to take place over the\nCWA. Backing up a touch to Thursday, potent system over the 4 \ncorners region should eject into the Great Plains and merge with a\npotent wave digging into the area within the main polar jet. This\nbecomes a potent wave across the eastern CONUS, driving a cold \nfront through the area into Saturday. Some timing differences \nexist across ensembles and the various deterministic/AI guidance, \nbut the general consensus seems to have this front through the \narea by Saturday morning with maybe the exception of far east \ncentral Georgia. This brings in much more seasonable air with \nhighs forecast in the 60s and 70s Saturday, and maybe even cooler \non Sunday as the cooler air mass continue to push into the region.\nGiven strong moisture convergence along the front, current precip\nchances may be a bit underdone across especially north Georgia, \nthough timing for something like this will be crucial. An \novernight frontal passage will be much more likely to be precip \nfree, for instance. This is still a week out, so worth keeping an \neye on.",
